          %h2#statement Statement
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Atiyah"} Michael Atiyah
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isadore+Singer"} Isadore Singer
            associated two differently defined numbers to an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+operator"} elliptic operator
            on a manifold: the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+index"} topological index
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytical+index"} analytical index
          %p
            The index theorem generalizes earlier results such as the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann-Roch+theorem"} Riemann-Roch theorem
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p The original articles:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Atiyah"}> Michael Atiyah
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isadore+Singer"} Isadore Singer
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The index of elliptic operators I
                %strong 87
                (1968) pp. 484–530;
                = succeed "," do
                  %em III
                %strong 87
                (1968) pp. 546–604;
                = succeed "," do
                  %em IV
                %strong 93
                (1971) pp. 119–138;
                = succeed "," do
                  %em V
                %strong 93
                (1971) pp. 139–149
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Atiyah"}> Michael Atiyah
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Graeme+Segal"} Graeme Segal
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The index of elliptic operators II
                %strong 87
                (1968) pp. 531–545
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li#HirzebruchGergerJung92
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Friedrich+Hirzebruch"}> Friedrich Hirzebruch
                , Thomas Berger, Rainer Jung, chapter 6 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Manifolds and Modular Forms
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 20
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-663-10726-2"} doi:10.1007/978-3-663-10726-2
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~v1ranick/papers/hirzjung.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Gilkey95
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Gilkey"}> Peter Gilkey
                , Section 5.2 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Invariance Theory: The Heat Equation and the Atiyah-Singer Index Theorem
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://pages.uoregon.edu/gilkey/dirPDF/InvarianceTheory1Ed.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                Rafe Mazzeo,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Atiyah-Singer Index theorem: what it is and why you should care
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://docplayer.net/52573131-The-atiyah-singer-index-theorem-what-it-is-and-why-you-should-care-rafe-mazzeo-october-10-2002.html"} slides
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Freed"}> Daniel Freed
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Atiyah-Singer index theorem
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://web.ma.utexas.edu/users/dafr/cmsa.pdf"} slides
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
